# --- Privy (required) -------------------------------------------------
#
# This app cannot run without a Privy App ID. Get one free in about a
# minute:
#
#   1. Go to https://dashboard.privy.io and sign up / log in.
#   2. Create a new app (any name).
#   3. Copy the "App ID" shown on the app's Settings page — it looks like
#      "clabcd1234efgh5678ijkl".
#   4. Paste it below.
#
# This value is public by design (the "NEXT_PUBLIC_" prefix means it is
# bundled into client-side JS) — that's how Privy is meant to be used.
# Do not put a secret/server-side Privy key in this file.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PRIVY_APP_ID=

# Privy app secret. SERVER ONLY — no NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, never sent to
# the browser. Dashboard -> Settings -> App secret.
#
# Required for `app/api/wallet/ensure/route.ts`, which verifies a caller's
# Privy access token and creates their embedded wallet server-side after
# login (see "Wallet provisioning" in README.md for why creation happens
# here instead of Privy's own login-time UI, and why this does NOT give
# this app's backend any signing authority over the resulting wallet).
PRIVY_APP_SECRET=

# --- Privy dashboard checklist (settings live in your Privy app, not here) --
#
# Where each value comes from: https://dashboard.privy.io -> your app ->
# Settings (Basics). NEXT_PUBLIC_PRIVY_APP_ID is PUBLIC (bundled into client
# JS). PRIVY_APP_SECRET is SECRET and server-only; never prefix it NEXT_PUBLIC_.
#
# [ ] Allowed origins (Configuration -> App settings): add the exact origin
#     with the port, http://localhost:3000, plus your production domain
#     (https://your-domain). http://localhost without the port does not match,
#     and Next.js silently moves to 3001 if 3000 is busy; free the port.
# [ ] Login methods (User management -> Authentication): email, Google, external wallet (matches loginMethods in
#     app/providers.tsx: email, google, wallet). Enable what you list.
#     Methods not enabled in the dashboard are hidden even if listed in code.
# [ ] Embedded wallets (Wallets): enable for Ethereum, create on login.
#     Coinbase Wallet is pinned to eoaOnly in code (it does not support
#     chain 97741): nothing to do in the dashboard.
#     WalletGate calls /api/wallet/ensure after login, which creates the wallet
#     server-side with PRIVY_APP_SECRET if the dashboard setting is off, so
#     both together is fine. TEE / user-controlled mode is what the live tests
#     used; the legacy device mode also works.
# [ ] Smart wallets (Wallets -> Smart wallets): NOT needed, leave off.
#     This template uses a plain embedded wallet. Enabling it is harmless.
#
# Verify any time: npm run doctor (or the pepu_check_privy_config MCP tool).

# --- Token gate: ERC-20 (optional, but see note below) -------------------
#
# The ERC-20 token address this app checks the signed-in wallet's balance
# of. Defaults below to WPEPU (Wrapped Pepe Unchained), the one token
# verified in @pepu-chain/sdk's token list — purely so this template runs and
# shows something meaningful out of the box. This is NOT a recommendation
# to gate your app on WPEPU specifically; replace it with your own
# token's address once you have one.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GATE_ERC20_ADDRESS=0xF9Cf4A16d26979b929Be7176bAc4e7084975FCB8

# --- Token gate: ERC-721 (optional, but see note below) -------------------
#
# The ERC-721 (NFT) collection address this app checks the signed-in
# wallet's balance of. Left blank by default: there is no verified NFT
# collection address on Pepe Unchained today, and this template will not
# invent one. Deploy the sibling `nft-mint` template (or your
# own ERC-721 contract), then paste its address here.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GATE_ERC721_ADDRESS=

# At least one of NEXT_PUBLIC_GATE_ERC20_ADDRESS /
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GATE_ERC721_ADDRESS must be a valid address for this app to
# run at all — see components/setup-gate.tsx.
